WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.392 --> 00:00:02.350
XIAOWEN XIN: All right.

00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.820
Hi, everyone.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:08.820
So my name is Xiaowen Xin, and
with me is Matt Silverlock.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.620
And we're really
excited to be here today

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.620
to talk about migrating and
modernizing your enterprise

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.990
workloads with
Migrate for Anthos.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.660
So I think Migrate for Anthos
is a really unique tool

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.070
on the market.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.640
And it can transform your
entire modernization strategy.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.180
But I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.530
So before we talk about
the what, let's first talk

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.090
about why.

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.770
Why do want to
modernize your workload?

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.030
And why do you want to run
them on GKE and Anthos?

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:38.530
So there are a
lot of reasons why

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:42.430
you might want to run your
workloads on GKE and Anthos.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.030
First, GKE and Anthos are
built upon Kubernetes.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.460
So you've heard a lot
about Kubernetes today.

00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:50.590
A lot of our customers
who use Kubernetes

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.040
are telling us that they're
seeing a 10x improvement

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.560
in the speed with which they
can deploy and provision

00:00:56.560 --> 00:00:58.670
their workloads.

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.660
In addition, our customers are
seeing much higher utilization,

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:05.540
leading to large double-digit
infrastructure savings.

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.190
And GKE built upon
this and provides

00:01:11.190 --> 00:01:13.770
an easy to use
simplified management

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:18.150
experience with features
such as node auto-repair,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.616
auto-upgrade, and auto-scale up.

00:01:21.616 --> 00:01:25.110
And Anthos takes that
even further and provides

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.400
an application
modernization platform

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.650
with a single pane
of glass for managing

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.190
all of your workloads,
whether they're on-prem,

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.320
on GCP, or on other clouds.

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:44.680
And of course, when you run your
workloads on GKE and Anthos,

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:47.290
you get access to the
best of GCP security

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.530
with features such as binary
authorization and container

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:53.330
analysis.

00:01:53.330 --> 00:01:57.730
And so all of these
benefits taken together

00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:02.380
gives our customers the ability
to move faster, be more agile,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.680
and be more competitive
in the marketplace.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.675
And so we see a lot of
customers want to modernize.

00:02:09.675 --> 00:02:13.460
But It's not easy
and large enterprises

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.200
have a lot of unique challenges.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.380
We have a lot of customers that
have hundreds of applications--

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:23.290
in many cases, they have
thousands of applications--

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.420
that were written
over the last 10, 15

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:29.080
years using legacy frameworks
running on legacy operating

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.230
systems.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:31.930
So what do you do with these?

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.410
It's not really practical
to rewrite all of them,

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:41.280
make them more cloud native, use
a microservices architecture.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:42.410
So what do you do?

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.200
Now, the good news here
is you don't actually

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.500
have to rewrite all of them.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:51.200
We see modernization
not as a binary problem.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.440
It's not all or nothing.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.700
And in fact, even though
your application is not

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.070
using a microservices
architecture,

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.530
you can still convert
them to using containers

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:03.345
and get a lot of
the same benefits.

00:03:03.345 --> 00:03:05.220
So this is where Migrate
for Anthos comes in.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.310
Migrate for Anthos
is a tool that

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:14.240
helps you extract your legacy
workloads from your VMs

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:18.990
and automatically containerize
them and run them on GKE.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:20.250
The tool is fast.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:21.750
It's easy to use.

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:23.710
And it does not require
rewriting your app.

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.260
That's a really key point.

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.390
So when you're done,
you have your workloads

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.040
running in containers.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.380
And you can leave the management
of the infrastructure,

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.610
of the underlying
infrastructure, to us.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.580
In effect, this reminds
me of a conversation

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:40.460
I had with a partner recently
who was really excited

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.960
about these features.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:48.050
He told me that VM management
is actually quite error prone

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:50.090
and a big burden for IT.

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:54.530
So if you are manually patching,
manually upgrading these VMs,

00:03:54.530 --> 00:03:58.730
then after a few months of doing
this, over your fleet of VMs,

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:00.620
you'll often see a
few VMs that have

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.438
errors, that run into errors.

00:04:02.438 --> 00:04:04.730
And what that means is it
also affects the applications

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.220
that are running on top of it.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:08.840
So after he learned
about Kubernetes,

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:11.210
or learned about
containers, he never

00:04:11.210 --> 00:04:15.110
wanted to go back to VM
management ever again.

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.420
And so VM management is--

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.360
the elimination of
OS and VM management

00:04:20.360 --> 00:04:22.305
is, of course, only one
of the many benefits.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.970
A customer of ours,
DevFactory, recently

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.550
used Migrate for Anthos and they
share their experience with us.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.380
So previously,
DevFactory was using--

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.300
they had a lot of
VMs and servers

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.310
running in a data center.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.060
And they were seeing
less than 1% utilization.

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.800
So these VMs were running there.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.760
They're costing money,
but they're not really

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.290
being fully used.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.260
So DevFactory decided to
use Migrate for Anthos

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:57.850
to move those workloads to GKE.

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:01.570
And immediately, they were able
to achieve much higher resource

00:05:01.570 --> 00:05:04.780
utilization, leading to
dramatically reduced costs.

00:05:07.285 --> 00:05:08.910
And also, they can
leave the management

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:10.860
of the infrastructure
to us, so they

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:13.850
can reduce their IT burden.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:17.243
The overall solution helped
them optimize their operations

00:05:17.243 --> 00:05:19.160
so that they can focus
on their core business.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:23.140
Well, OK.

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:26.260
So let's now dive a little
bit deeper into the solution

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:28.330
and see how it works.

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:32.620
At a high level, there
are three main steps.

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:36.100
The first step is to
identify good fit workloads.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.940
So we'll talk about
what good fit means

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:40.020
in a couple of slides.

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:43.440
Your source films can
be on-prem, on GCP,

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:47.710
on other clouds, and it
can be on physical servers.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:50.950
The operating system can be
any one of the major Linux

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.710
distributions.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.820
The next step is to migrate
using Migrate for Anthos.

00:05:56.820 --> 00:06:00.540
The entire migration can be
orchestrated directly from GKE,

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:03.390
will automatically
containerize your VM workloads,

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:05.482
and will move your
entire workload,

00:06:05.482 --> 00:06:07.440
including your code, your
data, and your state.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.550
So everything moves
over to a container.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:14.110
And finally, the last step
is to operate and optimize.

00:06:14.110 --> 00:06:15.860
So here, you want to
make sure that you've

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.420
set up your networking rules,
firewall rules, access control

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:21.050
quotas, and alerting and so on.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.500
And then you can enjoy
the benefits of a managed

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.200
infrastructure, including
increased utilization,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.680
elimination of OS managements,
integrated logging

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.792
and monitoring, and of
course, the entire stack

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:34.250
is supported and
managed by Google.

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:39.630
Let's all dive into
that second step, that

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:42.760
migrate step, a little
bit more to see how we're

00:06:42.760 --> 00:06:43.930
transforming your workload.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:49.690
So if you take your
original VM, there

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:51.550
are a lot of things
that are inside there.

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.440
You have your application.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:55.483
It has attached storage.

00:06:55.483 --> 00:06:57.400
You're doing your own
networking inside there.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:00.160
You have your DHCP
configuration, for example.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:03.040
You have operating system
services, like SHAD and DPD

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.500
and so on.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:08.140
The VM is managing lifecycle
of your application.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:11.830
It does its own logging, and you
have your own OS kernel drivers

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:14.130
all within that VM.

00:07:14.130 --> 00:07:17.370
So when we perform
that transformation,

00:07:17.370 --> 00:07:20.980
we put that into what we
call a system container.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:24.230
And you can see, at this
point, a lot of the components

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:28.490
in your original VM
have now been replaced

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:31.460
and are now managed
by Kubernetes.

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:34.760
So your storage is now
provided by persistent volumes

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.220
within Kubernetes.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:39.290
Networking is now also
managed by Kubernetes,

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:41.930
so you don't need your
own DHCP configuration.

00:07:41.930 --> 00:07:46.730
And it can seamlessly integrate
with services like Istio.

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:50.460
Operating system services
like SHAD, some of those stay

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:51.710
and some of those get removed.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:55.160
And that is configurable
by the user.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:59.120
The application lifecycle stays
within the system container.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.820
As far as logging, that's now
integrated with Kubernetes

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:02.910
and the cluster.

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:06.540
And so depending on your
cluster configuration,

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:10.202
you can have all of your logs
be sent directly to Stackdriver.

00:08:10.202 --> 00:08:11.910
And finally, your
operating system kernel

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:15.750
and drivers, those are no longer
managed within your workload.

00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:21.340
So this is what we do today.

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:23.850
Now, on a roadmap,
moving forward,

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.780
we have plans to break
this up even further.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.020
So we will split
your system container

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:32.169
into one or more application
containers, where

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:35.200
each container can now be-- each
application and container can

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.490
now be managed independently
and directly by Kubernetes.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.409
So the lifecycle management
of that container

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:43.710
will be managed
directly by Kubernetes.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:46.480
Kubernetes will be able to
independently scale the app up

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:49.960
and down, independently
manage the health monitoring

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:52.300
for the app, and so on.

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:55.390
And so as you can see, as
we go through the phases,

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:59.620
more and more of the
infrastructure bits

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:02.470
that was inside your
VM are now taken out,

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.840
unbundled from your
workload, and can

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:08.010
be managed independently
and centrally

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.956
within the infrastructure
within Kubernetes.

00:09:15.090 --> 00:09:17.460
So zooming back out,
a question that we

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:22.050
get a lot from our customers
is, what is a good fit workload?

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:24.580
What are sweet spot workloads?

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.600
So our solution is
actually very generic.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.480
And so we work very
well-- it works very well

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:33.390
for a lot of workloads.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.720
So common workloads
with an enterprise

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:42.140
like multi-tier LAMP
stacks or Java apps,

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.900
those are really good fits.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:48.110
If you have low duty cycle
and bursty workloads,

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:50.390
those also work really well,
because once you put them

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:53.450
into a container, then it's
easy to oversubscribe them.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:58.140
And you can very quickly
scale up and scale down.

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:01.470
So what's not a good
candidate workload?

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:04.790
So you want to make sure that
the workload will run well

00:10:04.790 --> 00:10:06.028
within Kubernetes.

00:10:06.028 --> 00:10:08.570
And so that means if you have
high performance or high memory

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.480
databases, that may
not be a good fit.

00:10:11.480 --> 00:10:14.205
If you have a special
kernel or hardware needs,

00:10:14.205 --> 00:10:16.330
like if you need a special
kernel module, that also

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:18.675
may not run within Kubernetes.

00:10:18.675 --> 00:10:20.050
And finally you
want to make sure

00:10:20.050 --> 00:10:23.800
that your licenses--
your software licenses--

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:25.910
allow you to run
within containers.

00:10:25.910 --> 00:10:28.090
So if you have licenses
that are tied to hardware ID

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:30.720
registration, that also
may not be a good fit.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:32.740
And in some of these
cases, you may instead

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:34.870
want to use Migrate
for Compute Engine

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:39.070
to move your VM from
your original cloud,

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:41.590
original source data
center into GCP.

00:10:44.590 --> 00:10:46.990
So another common question
we get from customers

00:10:46.990 --> 00:10:53.227
is, once I've migrated my VM,
my workload into the cloud,

00:10:53.227 --> 00:10:55.060
how do I make sure that
it's well connected?

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.160
How do I make sure it's
connected to my services

00:10:57.160 --> 00:10:58.550
that I need from on-prem?

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.460
How do I make sure that
on-prem can talk to it?

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:05.080
And also what if I have some
workloads that I can't yet

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:05.960
migrate to the cloud?

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:08.800
So how do I make sure
that everything can still

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.690
talk to each other?

00:11:10.690 --> 00:11:12.770
So to tell us more
about that, I'd

00:11:12.770 --> 00:11:14.020
like to turn it over to Matt.

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:14.520
Thank you.

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:22.860
MATT SILVERLOCK: Thank you.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:24.720
So what I want to
talk about is sort

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.060
of a couple of
customer examples where

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.400
they've had these
monolithic applications.

00:11:29.400 --> 00:11:31.860
And these cases,
actually, are sort of

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:33.970
before Anthos or Migrate
for Anthos came along,

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:36.095
so that would have saved
them a fair bit of effort.

00:11:36.095 --> 00:11:37.660
But they've got these monoliths.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:40.470
They start on-prem--
or even on the cloud,

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:41.490
but we'll say on-prem--

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:43.410
and they want to
bring them across.

00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:46.290
And they want to start
modernizing these applications.

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.630
They want to break them down.

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.390
They want to actually
help them scale.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.047
But that's actually really hard.

00:11:52.047 --> 00:11:53.880
It's hard to start
breaking these things up.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:56.172
And you want to do it in a
really, really low risk way.

00:11:56.172 --> 00:11:58.443
These are running-- it's
your e-commerce platform

00:11:58.443 --> 00:12:00.360
with billing integrated,
with cart integrated,

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:02.610
with webs that are front
end all integrated.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.010
Just ripping them out ad
hoc and rebuilding them

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:08.570
is not normally something
that people want to do,

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:11.860
because it's still
part of their business.

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:15.350
So we look at, first,
containerizing the application.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:18.595
I think that's probably one of
the most important first steps.

00:12:18.595 --> 00:12:19.970
That is obviously
a big challenge

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.178
for some legacy applications.

00:12:21.178 --> 00:12:23.060
But we have stuff
like, obviously,

00:12:23.060 --> 00:12:26.187
Migrate that helps make
that easier for Linux apps.

00:12:26.187 --> 00:12:28.520
And then the next part is
building a network foundation.

00:12:28.520 --> 00:12:29.937
So you want a
foundation where you

00:12:29.937 --> 00:12:33.690
can start to break this
app apart, comfortably

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:37.980
route traffic and users to
the broken out services,

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.050
and still make sure that
the stuff in the monolith

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:43.100
can still talk to those
broken out services,

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:44.850
that traffic can still
go to that monolith

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:47.520
when it's still
handling certain tasks.

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.740
In a way, again, that is
as low risk as possible,

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:52.410
and make sure you
initially roll back

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:56.250
or manage around those
changes, trace those changes,

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:57.935
debug those kind of issues.

00:12:57.935 --> 00:12:59.560
And then once you
have that foundation,

00:12:59.560 --> 00:13:02.018
you can start breaking it down
into microservices much more

00:13:02.018 --> 00:13:04.410
comfortably.

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:07.020
So as a bit of an overview
of the major components,

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:09.280
we've got containers themselves.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.210
We have a service mesh, which
means a lot of different things

00:13:12.210 --> 00:13:13.933
to a lot different people.

00:13:13.933 --> 00:13:16.100
But the idea is basically
to have your services talk

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:20.370
to each other in a controlled
and debuggable way.

00:13:20.370 --> 00:13:22.410
And Serverless--
so that's the idea

00:13:22.410 --> 00:13:24.620
that you're not running
sort of opposite a VM level

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:26.715
or even, in many ways,
a container level.

00:13:26.715 --> 00:13:28.590
You're just saying,
look, I want 10 of these.

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.330
I want to scale from zero to 50.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.570
And what we have a sort of
fits into a whole toolkit.

00:13:35.570 --> 00:13:39.950
So you can see we have Migrate
for Anthos on the left.

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:41.650
We have Kubernetes itself.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:44.110
We have GKE, GKE on-prem.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:47.220
You have this spectrum of what
we think as the Anthos service

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:47.720
mesh.

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:52.390
So Envoy Proxy-- so if you've
ever used Nginx before,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:56.360
Envoy is a super powered Nginx
with dynamic configuration

00:13:56.360 --> 00:14:00.160
that you can use a sidecar or as
a front proxy for applications

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:01.990
and do traffic shaping.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:04.630
Istio is a control plane.

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.240
A lot of things in Istio
is certificate management

00:14:07.240 --> 00:14:09.880
acting as a control plane
for your Envoy proxies

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:11.392
by the Envoy APIs.

00:14:11.392 --> 00:14:13.600
Traffic Director, which is
our version of the control

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.400
plane as a managed
service, since we

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:18.508
know that managing control
planes can be pretty hard.

00:14:18.508 --> 00:14:21.050
We also have a load balancer,
sort of internal load balancer,

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:24.030
for managing traffic between
services built on Envoy.

00:14:24.030 --> 00:14:26.842
gRPC is a communication
mechanism and Cloud Run,

00:14:26.842 --> 00:14:29.050
which sort of goes back to
the serverless part I just

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:29.770
mentioned before.

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:32.230
So these are all the components,
and it's like, OK, great.

00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:33.563
How do I put all these together?

00:14:33.563 --> 00:14:35.778
How do I actually even
know where to start?

00:14:35.778 --> 00:14:37.570
I'm not going to boil
the ocean and just do

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:38.403
all of this at once.

00:14:38.403 --> 00:14:40.983
That just doesn't
make a lot of sense.

00:14:40.983 --> 00:14:42.650
So again, we want to
containerize first,

00:14:42.650 --> 00:14:45.310
and then we want to start to
mesh applications and build up

00:14:45.310 --> 00:14:48.150
the service mesh so that it
iteratively and very slowly--

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.920
so we can make major
changes as we go along.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.090
So sort of classic
model of the gap--

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:58.000
it's a big, probably,
existing Java code base,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:14:59.365
lots of different modules.

00:14:59.365 --> 00:15:02.320
You probably deploy it maybe
once, maybe three or four times

00:15:02.320 --> 00:15:03.750
a year if you're really lucky.

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:06.250
But you want to actually be
able to sort of break this down.

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.040
You want teams to be able to
be independent of each other

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:11.650
to also avoid team
A from blowing up

00:15:11.650 --> 00:15:14.050
the application of
team B, so having

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:18.610
canary controls and things like
that as well, being a rollback.

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:21.890
And that sits on-prem, and you
just come indirectly or maybe

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:25.400
fire some sort of DNS traffic
management system if you've

00:15:25.400 --> 00:15:26.630
got a couple of data centers.

00:15:26.630 --> 00:15:30.860
So very simple, straightforward
setup with a lot of complexity

00:15:30.860 --> 00:15:32.720
underneath.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:35.030
And what we also want to
think about is like great,

00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:37.860
OK, let's start plugging
in these pieces.

00:15:37.860 --> 00:15:41.570
So I have Google's network
in front of my application.

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:44.660
That's putting up our global
load balancer with Anycast,

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:47.960
with DDoS protection,
with traffic routing,

00:15:47.960 --> 00:15:49.160
setting up an API gateway.

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:51.423
So this is the core
foundation where--

00:15:51.423 --> 00:15:53.840
today there might be a really
simple case of, just forward

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:55.040
everything to the monolith.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:57.440
But I just start thinking,
great, actually now

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:01.700
I can start carving up,
very easily, very carefully,

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:05.240
my card, my payment
system, my billing backend

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:07.610
with this gateway
ready, so that when

00:16:07.610 --> 00:16:11.720
I start to break
down this monolith,

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:13.080
I have those controls there.

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.380
And again-- I mean,
I say this a lot--

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.880
you can roll back carefully and
measure those changes really

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:18.103
carefully.

00:16:18.103 --> 00:16:20.770
And obviously, what you start to
do is you start build that out,

00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:22.353
and you get the
cluster ready on, say,

00:16:22.353 --> 00:16:25.125
GKE, which is going to be
your foundation for starting

00:16:25.125 --> 00:16:26.500
to break these
applications down.

00:16:26.500 --> 00:16:28.480
So you start to get
some experience in GKE.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:31.030
You start to understand how
it abstracts virtual machines

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:35.508
and how it manages the
network itself as well.

00:16:35.508 --> 00:16:37.800
So we're breaking down one
service, breaking down, say,

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:41.280
service A. That might be
our billing service or maybe

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.070
our cart service--

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:46.480
an API gateway that
processes, hey, we're

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.930
going to send a small fraction
of traffic over at a time.

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:51.150
And then as we're
happy with that, that's

00:16:51.150 --> 00:16:53.400
going to take all the
load off the monolith,

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:56.603
and our cart service is going
to exist as a true microservice.

00:16:56.603 --> 00:16:58.770
And the cart team is super
happy because now they're

00:16:58.770 --> 00:17:01.930
actually able to run
things on their own.

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:05.510
Again, that's really good
developer experience as well.

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:07.260
We are shrinking this
monolith bit by bit.

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:09.650
So now we have service
B. That might just

00:17:09.650 --> 00:17:10.700
be our web front end.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:13.010
So that serves as sort
of our user facing pages.

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:16.040
We've brought that across
now-- again, same process.

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:18.660
Using our load balancing
and the API gateway

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:23.060
we can make that a very
measured and careful change.

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.520
And now we're able to rely
on Kubernetes and GKE,

00:17:25.520 --> 00:17:28.020
specifically, to do
auto-scaling of those services

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:29.645
now that we've broken them out.

00:17:29.645 --> 00:17:32.270
We can scale them independently
of each other and independently

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:34.562
of the monolith that they
rely on, or we're relying on.

00:17:37.007 --> 00:17:38.090
We can get a little fancy.

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:40.417
We can start to use gRPC.

00:17:40.417 --> 00:17:42.000
Obviously, reduced
the overheads on it

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:46.010
so the traffic coming from our
gateway back to our services

00:17:46.010 --> 00:17:47.370
reduces latency in many cases.

00:17:47.370 --> 00:17:49.932
So we can start to
iteratively and carefully

00:17:49.932 --> 00:17:51.890
bring in these additional
modernization efforts

00:17:51.890 --> 00:17:55.240
as well as we can
break them all down.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:57.420
But hey, maybe there
are some services

00:17:57.420 --> 00:17:59.610
you do want to keep on-prem.

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:02.280
There are services and, as
others have discussed before,

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:03.758
regulatory reasons.

00:18:03.758 --> 00:18:04.800
Maybe complexity reasons.

00:18:04.800 --> 00:18:08.250
Maybe there are just truly a lot
of dependencies that are there.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.440
Maybe there's specific
hardware requirements

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:14.145
that we can't get in the
cloud maybe just today

00:18:14.145 --> 00:18:16.020
or existing investments
you want to leverage.

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:17.070
You're like, you know what?

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:18.445
I'm going to break
out service C,

00:18:18.445 --> 00:18:19.830
but I'm going to run it on-prem.

00:18:19.830 --> 00:18:22.380
And GKE on-prem is for Anthos.

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:23.460
Keep that there.

00:18:23.460 --> 00:18:27.000
But hey, how does it talk
to the other micro-services?

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.390
How does it discover
those things?

00:18:29.390 --> 00:18:31.540
So what we can start
to build in is using,

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:32.790
sort of, our Envoy Proxies.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:35.600
This is the first step
towards our service mesh.

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:39.090
So in many ways, if you haven't
been familiar with Envoy

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:41.642
before, the way you'd
normally set it up is,

00:18:41.642 --> 00:18:43.600
in a container system,
you set it as a sidecar.

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:47.560
So all traffic coming into that
container or those containers

00:18:47.560 --> 00:18:49.990
as pods in Kubernetes,
it intercepts that,

00:18:49.990 --> 00:18:52.480
is effectively sort
of a reverse and front

00:18:52.480 --> 00:18:55.750
proxy for all traffic coming
into that application.

00:18:55.750 --> 00:18:57.580
You can do canarying
through that.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:00.550
You can have multiple
versions set up.

00:19:00.550 --> 00:19:03.250
And you can do some stuff like
MTLS or mutual TLS as well

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:04.810
and secure and
authenticate traffic

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.840
going between these services so
that service B and C can talk

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:09.340
to each other, but
B should not talk

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:13.937
to, say, A, if we had
an Envoy there as well.

00:19:13.937 --> 00:19:15.520
Starts to build out
a little bit more.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:18.170
So what we might have is
we have a control point.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:20.425
So that's the service mesh
CP that you can see here.

00:19:20.425 --> 00:19:22.300
We need something to
orchestrate our proxies.

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:24.490
Maybe initially they had
a fairly static config

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:26.110
that we just built
into a config map,

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:28.087
and we deployed it as needed.

00:19:28.087 --> 00:19:29.420
We didn't make a lot of changes.

00:19:29.420 --> 00:19:31.720
But we're realizing,
hey, our operators

00:19:31.720 --> 00:19:34.210
need to be able to roll
this out, again, carefully.

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:36.250
They want to be able to
make changes dynamically

00:19:36.250 --> 00:19:38.710
without actually
redeploying the application.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:41.170
That can, obviously, be very
easy sometimes in Kubernetes.

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:42.355
But if it's a very
stateful application

00:19:42.355 --> 00:19:44.105
with a lot of dependencies,
it's something

00:19:44.105 --> 00:19:46.120
you want to take care of.

00:19:46.120 --> 00:19:49.150
And you might have a control
point on-prem as well

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:50.530
for managing the envoys there.

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:54.640
So you have these two
divorced control planes that

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:56.050
do a lot of the same thing.

00:19:56.050 --> 00:19:58.920
And you can probably see
where I'm going with this.

00:19:58.920 --> 00:20:02.490
And what we wanted to get to is
what we call Traffic Director.

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:06.037
So today Traffic Director
works through GCP directly,

00:20:06.037 --> 00:20:08.620
and we're building this out to
have a hybrid approach as well.

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:11.567
So you can have a
control plane on GCP

00:20:11.567 --> 00:20:13.650
managing all of your service
meshes on Kubernetes.

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:15.442
You can have it manage
these service meshes

00:20:15.442 --> 00:20:17.710
in other clusters,
be they on-prem,

00:20:17.710 --> 00:20:19.560
be they on other cloud
providers as well.

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:21.740
That's really the dream
we're going to get to.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:23.850
So you have this unified control
point that your developers

00:20:23.850 --> 00:20:24.460
interact with.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:27.290
And wherever those
clusters are living--

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:29.310
and I definitely
recommend having

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:32.647
definitely more than one
cluster for blast radius--

00:20:32.647 --> 00:20:34.230
you can manage all
those as if they're

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:36.188
one large unit and making
sure that application

00:20:36.188 --> 00:20:40.527
is on, say, platform cluster
A doing, say, your Jupyter

00:20:40.527 --> 00:20:43.110
notebook management, something
like that, or some data science

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:46.880
jobs, can talk to the cluster
running maybe some batch

00:20:46.880 --> 00:20:47.970
training jobs as well.

00:20:47.970 --> 00:20:51.450
So you want to have that
cross-cluster communication

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:53.400
but with a centralized
control point as well.

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.180
And this is the
hybrid approach here.

00:20:58.180 --> 00:21:01.770
So having this singular control
plane as much as possible

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:04.260
either abstracts the need to
run a control plane on-prem

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:07.770
or controls the on-prem control
plane so that, maybe in a worst

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:10.560
case scenario, if there
was a connectivity issue

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:13.278
between on-prem and
reaching out to the Cloud,

00:21:13.278 --> 00:21:14.820
you have a local
control plane that's

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:16.797
going to continue to
operate for as long

00:21:16.797 --> 00:21:17.880
as it needs to on its own.

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:22.828
So as a bit of a
recap, you can see

00:21:22.828 --> 00:21:24.370
we've started off
with this monolith,

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:26.453
and we've started to break
it down piece by piece.

00:21:26.453 --> 00:21:28.730
So we're taking this
really carefully.

00:21:28.730 --> 00:21:30.880
We've built out our load
balancer and a gateway

00:21:30.880 --> 00:21:32.350
as a preparation
and a foundation

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:33.940
for how you want to do this.

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:36.010
And that gives us,
again, really useful

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.290
capabilities for
canarying traffic,

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:41.350
for splitting traffic
out, for doing traffic

00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:43.168
on RPS and least connections.

00:21:43.168 --> 00:21:45.460
And based on, actually, the
load of my backend as well.

00:21:45.460 --> 00:21:48.850
I can obviously keep going
down that path with my gateway.

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:51.220
I've been able to
implement gRPC to reduce

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:53.830
overheads and increase
security on, say,

00:21:53.830 --> 00:21:56.210
connection to other services
that support that as well.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:58.120
So I've made those
changes carefully.

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.670
Service A starts gRPC.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:02.430
I've built gRPC to
serve as B. I don't

00:22:02.430 --> 00:22:05.860
have to, again, bore the
ocean, do it all at once.

00:22:05.860 --> 00:22:10.520
And then I've broken out the
monoliths into finally as a--

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:13.990
we'll say, service C here, but
it could be services C, D, E, F

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:15.760
on-prem.

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:18.320
And I've had this
universal service

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:21.430
that talks to all applications
and manages the proxies,

00:22:21.430 --> 00:22:24.780
manages those sidecars for
me as much as possible.

00:22:24.780 --> 00:22:27.340
Because, again, what
we are getting to here

00:22:27.340 --> 00:22:32.665
is we don't want to be running
all this infrastructure that's,

00:22:32.665 --> 00:22:34.540
in many cases, adjacent
to the business goals

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:35.290
we want to get to.

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:38.660
Like, most of you
here have managed VMs,

00:22:38.660 --> 00:22:42.222
and now we have the cloud, which
abstracts a lot of that mostly.

00:22:42.222 --> 00:22:43.180
And we have Kubernetes.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:46.190
But Kubernetes has a
large number of APIs.

00:22:46.190 --> 00:22:48.485
And if you want to start
configuring, say, Envoy,

00:22:48.485 --> 00:22:49.860
go ahead and look
at Envoy Proxy.

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:51.462
Have a look at the config.

00:22:51.462 --> 00:22:53.170
Have a look at how
you would manage that.

00:22:53.170 --> 00:22:54.740
And you can imagine
a fleet of those.

00:22:54.740 --> 00:22:55.823
You get this config group.

00:22:55.823 --> 00:22:58.750
So control planes like
Istio, control planes that

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:01.810
live like Traffic
Director abstract the need

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:03.760
to have to do those
hands-on management.

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:04.990
And you can keep everything
a little bit more

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.270
centralized, particularly
from a security or ops team

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:09.660
perspective.

00:23:09.660 --> 00:23:10.980
So takeaways?

00:23:10.980 --> 00:23:12.690
Containerize first.

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.000
Definitely recommend that.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:17.590
So that would be--
again, Migrate for Anthos

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:18.750
is a great first start.

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:20.792
And maybe if you have some
Windows apps or things

00:23:20.792 --> 00:23:22.793
like that can't fit
into that today,

00:23:22.793 --> 00:23:24.960
those are processes you
maybe iteratively break that

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:27.190
out yourself.

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:30.780
De-risk your migrations by
breaking them out piecemeal

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:32.850
and having a plan
for each piece,

00:23:32.850 --> 00:23:36.090
having a plan that involves
the network so you can make

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:38.640
those changes quickly, again,
roll back those changes,

00:23:38.640 --> 00:23:41.027
and critically
monitor error rates,

00:23:41.027 --> 00:23:43.110
trace where traffic's
going, trace the performance

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:46.530
of your newly containerized
and broken out applications.

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:49.165
And leverage service meshes to
bridge on-prem with the cloud.

00:23:49.165 --> 00:23:51.540
If you're going to be keeping
your on-prem infrastructure

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:53.470
or have a multi-cloud
environment as well,

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:55.805
you want to be leveraging
service meshes to make sure

00:23:55.805 --> 00:23:57.930
that these things can
communicate where they should

00:23:57.930 --> 00:24:00.440
communicate, where they don't
communicate where they should.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.400
So you don't want a PCI
environment, for example,

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.108
communicating with a
non-PCI environment.

00:24:04.108 --> 00:24:06.990
That's a horrible ordered scope.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:10.617
Using a service mesh to prevent
that at the networking layer

00:24:10.617 --> 00:24:11.700
can make that really easy.

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:12.700
You can have fire rules.

00:24:12.700 --> 00:24:14.425
You can have a service
mesh constraints.

00:24:14.425 --> 00:24:16.050
You can have MTLS
constraints and layer

00:24:16.050 --> 00:24:19.760
those on to get multiple
layers of protection.

